</section>
</section>


<section>
  <note class="self-check"><para>Solve the system of equations using elimination.</para>
    <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}7x\;–\;2y\;=\;3\\4x\;+\;5y\;=\;3.25\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  </note>
</section>

<section>
  <title>Additional Resources</title>
  <section>
    <title>Solving a System of Equations by Elimination Involving Fractions</title>
  <para>You just ordered the steps for solving a system of equations using elimination. Try
    using these steps when the system has equations containing fractions.</para>
  <para>When the system of equations contains fractions, we will first clear the fractions by
    multiplying each equation by the least common denominator (LCD) of all the
    fractions in the equation.</para>
  <para>Solve the system by elimination: \( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} x + \frac{1}{2}y =6\\ \frac{3}{2}x + \frac{2}{3}y = \frac{17}{2} \end{array}\right. \)</para>
  
  <!--BEGIN STEPS-->
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Check to see if each equation is in standard form \(Ax+By=C\).
    Both equations are in standard form.</para>
    <para>
    \(x+\frac12 y=6\)
    \(\frac32x + \frac23 y=\frac{17}{2} \) 
    </para>

   <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> To clear the fractions, multiply each equation by its LCD (Least Common Denominator).</para>
   <para> 
   \(2(x+\frac12 y=6)\)
    \(6(\frac32 x+\frac23 y=\frac{17}{2})\)
   </para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Simplify the equations.</para>
    <para> 
    \(2x+y=12\)
    \(9x+4y=51\)
  </para>
   <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Eliminate one of the variables and simplify the equation.</para>
    <para>\(-4(2x+y=12)=- 8x-4y=-48\)</para>

   <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Add the two equations to eliminate \(y\).</para>
    <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->
    <para>
    \(\begin{align*}-8x-4y&amp;=-48
    \\9x+4y&amp;=51
    \\ \hline
     x&amp;=3
    \end{align*}\)
  </para>
  <!--End ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->	
  <para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Solve for \(y\) by substituting \(x\) back into one of the equations.</para>
  <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS -->
  <para>
  \(\begin{align*}2x+y&amp;=12
  \\2(3)+y&amp;=12
  \\6+y&amp;=12
  \\y&amp;=6
  \\\end{align*}\)
  </para>
  <!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->		
  <para><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Write the solution as an ordered pair.</para>
  <para>The ordered pair is \((3,6)\).</para>

  <para><emphasis>Step 8 -</emphasis> Check that the ordered pair is a solution to <emphasis>both</emphasis> original equations.</para>
   <!--BEGIN SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS-->
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rr}
      &amp; \frac32 x+\frac23 y= \frac{17}{2}	\\
    x+\frac12 y \overset?=6 &amp; \frac32 (3)+\frac23 (6) \overset?= \frac{17}{2}\\
    3+\frac12 (6) \overset?= 6&amp;\frac92 +4 \overset?=\frac{17}{2}	\\
    3+3\overset?=6 &amp; \frac92 + \frac82 \overset?=\frac{17}{2} \\  
    6=6\checkmark &amp; \frac{17}{2}=\frac{17}{2}\checkmark
    \end{array}\)</para>
    <!--END SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS -->
   
   <para>The solution is \((3,6)\).</para>
  <!-- END STEPS -->

  <note class="try look-at-me">
  <title>Try It: Solving a System of Equations by Elimination Involving Fractions</title>

  <exercise>
    <problem>
      <para>Solve the following system of equations.</para>      
      <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}\frac13\;x\;-\;\frac12y\;=\;1\\\frac34x\;\;-\;y\;=\;\frac52\end{array}\right.\)</para>
    
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
     <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Check to see if each equation is in standard form \(Ax+By=C\).
      Both equations are in standard form.</para>
      <para>\(\frac13x-\frac12y=1\\\frac34x-y=\frac52\)</para>
     <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> To clear the fractions, multiply each equation by its LCD (Least Common Denominator).</para>
       <para>\({\style{color:red}6}(\frac13x-\frac12y) ={\style{color:red}6}(1)\\{\style{color:red}4}(\frac34x-y)={\style{color:red}4}(\frac52)\)</para>

      <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Simplify the equations.</para>
      <para>
      \(2x-3y=6\)
      \(3x-4y=10\)
    </para>

     <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> To get equivalent coefficients of \(x\), we will need to multiply the first equation by 3 and the second equation by 2.</para>
     <para>
      \({\style{color:red}3} (2 x – 3 y ) = {\style{color:red}3} ( 6 ) \)
         \({\style{color:red}2} (3 x – 4 y ) = {\style{color:red}2} ( 10 )\) 
        </para>

     <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Simplify.</para>
     <para>
      \(6 x – 9 y = 18\)
      \(6 x – 8 y = 20\)
    </para>

     <para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Subtract the equations to eliminate one variable.</para>
      <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->		
      <para>
      \(\begin{align*}6x-9y&amp;=18
      \\6x-8y&amp;=20
      \\ \hline
      -y&amp;=-2
      \\y&amp;=2
      \end{align*}\)
    </para>
      <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS WITH LINE-->	
     <para><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Solve for \(x\) by substituting \(y\) back into one of the equations.</para>
      <!--BEGIN ALIGN TO EQUALS -->	
      <para>
      \(\begin{align*}2x-3y&amp;=6
      \\2x-3(2)&amp;=6
      \\2x-6&amp;=6
      \\2x&amp;=12
      \\x&amp;=6
      \\\end{align*}\)
    </para>
      <!--END ALIGN TO EQUALS -->		
     <para><emphasis>Step 8 -</emphasis> Write the solution as an ordered pair.</para>
      <para>The ordered pair is \((6,2)\).</para>

     <para><emphasis>Step 9 -</emphasis> Check that the ordered pair is a solution to <emphasis>both</emphasis> original equations.	</para>
        
        <!--BEGIN SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS-->
      
      <para>\(\begin{array}{rr}
        &amp; \frac34 -y = \frac{5}{2}	\\
          &amp; \frac34 (6)-2= \frac52	\\
        \frac13 x-\frac12 \overset?=1 &amp; \frac{18}{4}-2 \overset?= \frac52 \\
        \frac13 (6)\frac12 (6) \overset?= 6&amp;\frac{18}{4} -\frac84 \overset?=\frac52	\\
        2-1\overset?=1 &amp; \frac92 + \frac{10}{4} \overset?= \frac52 \\  
        1=1\checkmark &amp; \frac52=\frac52 \checkmark
        \end{array}\)</para>
        
        <!--END SIDE BY SIDE EQUATIONS -->
    
    <para>The solution is \((6,2)\).</para>
  </solution>
</exercise>
</note>
  </section>
</section>
